
Array in C

An 'array' is a secondary (derived) data type: a collection of similar-type data stored in 'contiguous memory locations'.


 Why use arrays?
When we have to work on a large amount of data, arrays let us store and process (handle) it in an easier and smarter way, instead of declaring many separate variables.

 Syntax :        
              datatype var_name[ele];     // elements

`ele` = number (count) of data items we are storing in the array.
           

int a[5];      // a is an array of 5 ints
char s[10];    // s is an array of 10 chars


 Key points

- Data stored in an array is called an 'array element'.
- Use the - index operator `[]` to access array elements.
- Array index always starts at 0.
- Total size = number of elements x size of one element (e.g. 5 x 4 = 20 bytes for `int a[5]`).

 Memory layout of  int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};


| Index             | a[0] | a[1] | a[2] | a[3] | a[4] |
| Value             | 10   | 20   | 30   | 40   | 50   |
| Address (example) | 1000 | 1004 | 1008 | 1012 | 1016 |


 Example: accessing elements

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

    printf("%zu\n", sizeof(a[0]));   // 4
    printf("%d\n", a[0]);            // 10
    printf("%d\n", a[2]);            // 30

    a[2]++;
    printf("%d\n", a[2]);            // 31

    printf("%zu\n", sizeof(a));      // 20 (bytes) = 4 x 5

    return 0;
}


/// Printing all elements


#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        printf("%d\n", a[i]);

    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}

Output:

10
20
30
40
50


Without a loop, this is the same as writing `printf("%d\n", a[0]);` ... `printf("%d\n", a[4]);` one by one.


/// Printing addresses of elements

printf("%p\n", (void *)&a[i]);   // prints address of each element

/// Scan and print array elements

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a[5];
    int i, ele;

    ele = sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0]);   // 5

    printf("Enter an array elements:\n");
    for (i = 0; i < ele; i++)
        scanf("%d", &a[i]);

    for (i = 0; i < ele; i++)
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}

Input: 10 20 30 40 50
Output: 10 20 30 40 50


 Valid and invalid declarations

int a[5];                          // valid
int a[];                           // invalid (index can't be empty)
int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};    // valid (compiler decides size: 5 elements, 20 bytes)


---> Array initialization

 //Full initialization

int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};   // valid


/// Excess elements

int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70};

- Excess elements in the array initializer are not stored.
- Only the first 5 are accepted (most compilers give a warning).


--> Partial initialization

int a[5] = {10, 20};         // 10, 20, 0, 0, 0

- Remaining elements are set to '0'.

 -->  Size decided by user input (dynamic declaration)

int ele;
printf("Enter no of elements:\n");
scanf("%d", &ele);
int a[ele];                   // dynamic declaration (variable length array)


- Some compilers accept this, some give an error.
- The size of the array (in bytes) is decided based on the user input.

/// Printing elements in reverse

#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int a[5];
    int i, ele = 5;

    printf("Enter an array elements:\n");
    for (i = 0; i < ele; i++)
        scanf("%d", &a[i]);

    for (i = ele - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}


For  ele = 5 and array 10 20 30 40 50: output is 50 40 30 20 10.


 Common mistake

for (i = ele; i > 0; i--)
    printf("%d ", a[i]);


This starts at `a[5]`, which is out of bounds (garbage value first), and it never prints `a[0]`. Output looks like `<garbage> 40 30 20 10`.

Correct loop: `for (i = ele - 1; i >= 0; i--)`

 Quick summary

| Concept            | Detail                                               |

| Definition         | Collection of similar-type data in contiguous memory |
| Index              | Starts at 0, last is `size - 1`                      |
| Size in bytes      | `sizeof(a)`                                          |
| Number of elements | `sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0])`                           |
| Partial init       | Remaining elements become 0                          |
| Empty size `[]`    | Only valid with an initializer                       |

 
